WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.070
Rock cleavage is a tendency to split along closely spaced surfaces

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.590
produced by deformation and mineral alignment.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:09.730
It may cut across the original bedding.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:14.310
Slate is a fine-grained rock that commonly has well-developed cleavage.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:19.170
Foliation is a planar fabric expressed by aligned minerals or compositional

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:23.130
layers. Some rocks split into thin sheets, while others show coarse

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:27.670
light and dark bands. Distinguish this metamorphic fabric from depositional bedding

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:29.150
that may still be visible.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:31.610
Lineation is a linear fabric:

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:36.610
elongated minerals, stretched objects, or intersections of planar features align along

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.630
a direction. It is not the same geometric feature as a

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:44.610
planar foliation. Different lineations reflect different processes and should be described

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:45.410
before interpretation.

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:50.610
Relict bedding, clasts, and other features can survive metamorphism and record

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:54.350
the parent rock. Minerals can rotate, grow, and become elongated during

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:58.810
deformation. Compare these younger fabrics with preserved older structures rather than

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:00.150
assuming all lines are bedding.
